{
  "rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are close in the first ~1–7 days, but become increasingly too low from ~day 10 onward (systematic underprediction in rising regimes). Apply an upward drift correction that grows with horizon (e.g., +0.1 to +0.2 €/day after day 7, capped).",
    "Trend/lag error: model reacts too slowly to regime shifts (misses sustained rebounds). If the last 5–10 days show a positive slope, add a trend-following adjustment to the whole path (proportional to recent slope), especially for days 10–30.",
    "Spike underestimation: after sharp upside moves in the realized series, the model tends to fade them too quickly and undershoots subsequent levels. When a 1–3 day jump exceeds a threshold, reduce mean-reversion strength and lift days 5–20 by a fixed premium.",
    "Level anchoring too low: forecasts appear anchored to the recent local mean around low-60s even when the market transitions to mid/high-60s. Use a blended anchor: increase weight on the most recent 3–5 day average vs the 30-day mean when recent prices are accelerating upward.",
    "Late-horizon compression: forecast volatility is too low at 20–30d, missing higher terminal outcomes. Inflate the distribution/trajectory range (wider bands) and raise the terminal level when recent realized volatility has increased."
  ]
}
